 reform or infringement on workers’ rights? EDITOR PICKS Keep the fight against Polio alive October 25, 2026 Ethiopia’s debt crisis: beyond the billions October 18, 2026 A cabinet must deliver, not merely govern October 11, 2026 POPULAR POSTS The Easter holiday amid fuel shortages: Sacrificing tomorrow for today’s meal April 11, 2026 Djibouti’s Presidential Election 2026: A vote of confidence in three decades of transformation April 11, 2026 Lawyers push for massive $58 billion compensation for Ethiopian Airlines crash victims’ families August 9, 2024 POPULAR CATEGORY AfricaNews 4580 Local News 2727 ispot 2199 Capital News 1382 In Pictures 1236 Sports 1155 News 1139 Arts and Culture 665 ABOUT US Capital is the longest-established private English newspaper in Ethiopia Contact us: info@capitalethiopia.com +251911226900 | +251911637862 | +251944732300 | +251116183253 FOLLOW US Facebook Instagram Linkedin Telegram TikTok Twitter © Newspaper WordPress Theme by TagDiv Home Capital News Editorial Interview Society Opinion Business & Economy More አማርኛ",
      "summary": "El Niño is posing a significant threat to global food security, despite recent gains in wheat production. The phenomenon, characterized by warmer ocean temperatures in the Pacific, is expected to disrupt weather patterns, leading to adverse effects on crop yields. While wheat production has seen improvements in some regions, the overall impact of El Niño could negate these gains, particularly in vulnerable areas. Experts warn that the increased likelihood of droughts and floods could severely affect agricultural output, exacerbating food shortages and inflation. Countries heavily reliant on wheat imports may face heightened risks, leading to potential social unrest. As nations prepare for the upcoming El Niño event, there is an urgent need for strategic planning and investment in agricultural resilience to mitigate the impending challenges to food security.",
